<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Roxas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Roxas was the first president of the independent Republic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, taking office in 1946 after serving as the last president of the Commonwealth. He led reconstruction after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and signed agreements giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> trade privileges and military bases. He died in office in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-roxas.jpg" alt="Manuel Roxas" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hessler Studio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Roxas_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel Roxas was born on 1 January 1892 in Capiz, now Roxas City, a posthumous child: his father had been mortally wounded by the Spanish Guardia Civil the year before. His mother and her father raised him. He graduated from Manila High School with honours in 1909, took a law degree at the University of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1913 as class valedictorian and topped the bar examinations that year with 92 per cent.</p><p>He served on the municipal council of Capiz from 1917 to 1919 and as its provincial governor from 1919 to 1922, the youngest the province had had. Elected to the Philippine House of Representatives in 1922, he was Speaker for twelve consecutive years. He also sat in the Constitutional Convention of 1934, served as secretary of finance and held a commission as a brigadier general in the United States Army Forces in the Far East.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Elected to the Senate in 1941, Roxas could not serve until 1945. After the fall of Corregidor he was captured by Japanese forces and held at Malaybalay and Davao, where the officer Nobuhiko Jimbo failed to persuade him to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-executive-commission">Philippine Executive Commission</a> and later shielded him from an order to execute him. He became chief adviser to the collaborationist government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-p-laurel">José P. Laurel</a> and surrendered to American forces at Baguio in April 1945.</p><p>In 1946 he left the Nacionalista Party with Elpidio Quirino to form the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a>. With the support of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douglas-macarthur">Douglas MacArthur</a> he won the presidential election on 23 April 1946 with 54 per cent of the vote. He was last president of the Commonwealth from 28 May to 4 July 1946, then first president of the Third Republic when independence was proclaimed before some 300,000 people at Luneta Park. Congress ratified the Treaty of General Relations on 5 August 1946, which recognised independence and reserved bases for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The country faced near bankruptcy after the war. His administration was marred by graft and by the abuses of the provincial military police, which contributed to the rise of the Huk movement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 15 April 1948, after a speech to the Thirteenth Air Force at Clark Air Base, Roxas suffered dizziness and fatigue and died that night of multiple heart attacks at the age of 56. Vice President Quirino took the oath as president on 17 April, and Roxas was buried at the Manila North Cemetery.</p><p>Several towns were renamed for him, beginning with Roxas, Oriental Mindoro, in 1948, as was Roxas City, Capiz, in 1951. Dewey Boulevard in Metro Manila was renamed in his memory, and he appeared on the 100 peso bill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel Roxas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its first president, <b>Manuel Roxas</b>, took office amid wartime devastation, and the new republic signed trade and military base agreements that kept it closely tied to America.</blockquote><a href="/state/philippines">Philippines</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Capiz, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=11.38,122.63&amp;year=1928" aria-label="Capiz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuel Roxas"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5221 4033h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5221 4033h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=11.38,122.63&amp;year=1928">What was here in 1928? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
